    "blocker": null,
    "examples": [
      {
        "question_text": "Classify each numerical variable as discrete or continuous. Give a reason.\n(a) The number of books borrowed from a library in a day.\n(b) The mass of a parcel.",
        "solution_text": "(a) **Discrete**: books are counted in whole steps.\n(b) **Continuous**: mass is measured and can take any value in a range."
      },
      {
        "question_text": "Classify each categorical variable as nominal or ordinal. Give a reason.\n(a) Favourite ice cream flavour.\n(b) Responses to a survey: disagree, neutral, agree.",
        "solution_text": "(a) **Nominal**: flavours have no natural order.\n(b) **Ordinal**: the responses have a natural order from disagree to agree."
      },
      {
        "question_text": "Classify each recorded variable and explain your choice.\n(a) A bus route number used to identify the route.\n(b) Time taken to finish a race, measured in seconds and rounded to two decimal places.\n(c) Age recorded as the number of completed years.",
        "solution_text": "(a) **Categorical nominal**: the number labels a route. It is not a counted or measured amount, and the route labels have no natural order.\n(b) **Numerical continuous**: time is a measurement that can take any value in a range. Rounding the measurement does not make time a count.\n(c) **Numerical discrete**: the recorded number of completed years increases in whole steps. Age considered as the time since birth is a continuous measurement."
      },
      {
        "question_text": "Explain why each classification is incorrect and give the correct subtype.\n(a) A student says that meal sizes labelled small, medium and large are nominal because they are words.\n(b) A student says that the number of spectators at a sporting final is continuous because the crowd is very large.",
        "solution_text": "(a) **Categorical ordinal**: small, medium and large have a natural order. Word labels can represent ordered categories.\n(b) **Numerical discrete**: spectators are counted in whole steps. A large count still has separated possible values."
      }
    ],
